Let $\Omega$ be an open set of $\R^d$.
In this section, we prove that $C_0^oo(\Omega)$ is a separable topological vector space.
Before studing that space, we instead study $C_0^oo(K)$ where $K$ is a compact subset
of $\R^d$.

First of all, clearly $C_0^oo(K)$ is a vector space.
We define a seminorm $p_\alpha$ for each multiindex $\alpha$ on $C_0^oo(K)$ by
$$
    p_\alpha(\phi) \defeq \sup_{x\in K}|D^\alpha\phi(x)|.
$$

Consider $q_n(x) = c_n(1-|x|^2)^n$ such that $\int_{|x|<=1}q_n(x)dx = 1$.
Using the polar coorinate, we can obtain
$$
    1 = \int_{|x|<=1}c_n(1-|x|^2)^n dx =
    \int_0^1\int_{|x|=r}c_n(1-r^2)^nd\sigma dr = N(d)\int_0^1 c_n(1-r^2)^n
    r^{d-1}dr.
$$ 
Here, if $d=1$, we obtain $c_n <= \sqrt{n}$, and if $d>=2$, then $c_n <= Nn$
where $N=N(d)$ is a constant.
So define $\kappa_1(n) = \sqrt{n}$ and $\kappa_d(n)=Nn$ for $d>=2$ to write
simply.

For given $f\in C_0^oo(K)$, deifne
$$
    f_n(x) \defeq \int_{|y|<=1} q_n(y)f(x-y)dy.
$$
One can easily notice that $f_n$ is a polynomial, and since $f$ is infinitely
differentiable,
$$
    D^\alpha f_n(x) \defeq \int_{|y|<=1} q_n(y)(D_x^\alpha f)(x-y)dy.
$$

Now fix $\epsilon>0$ and a positive integer $k$. Since $K+B[0,1]$ is compact, we
can take $\delta\in(0,1)$ such that $|D^\alpha f(x) - D^\alpha f(x-y)|<\epsilon$
if $|y|<\delta$ for every $x\in K$, and multiindex $\alpha$ with $|\alpha|<=k$.
Then we obtain
useenv align* {
    \sup_{x\in K}|D^\alpha f(x) - D^\alpha f_n(x)|
    &<= \sup_{x\in K}\int_{|y|<=1}q_n(y)|D^\alpha f(x-y) - D^\alpha f_n(x-y)|dy
    \\
    &<= 2 ?(\sup_{y\in K+B[0,1]}|D^\alpha f(y) )?\int_{\delta<= |y|<=1}q_n(y)dy
    + \epsilon\int_{|y|<\delta}q_n(y)dy \\
    &<= 2 ?(\sup_{y\in K+B[0,1]}|D^\alpha f(y) )?\int_{\delta<=
    |y|<=1}\kappa_d(n)(1-\delta^2)^ndy + \epsilon \\
    &= N(K,d,k,\delta,f)\kappa_d(n)(1-\delta^2)^n+\epsilon \\
    &-> \epsilon
}
as $n->oo$ because $0 < 1-\delta^2 < 1$ and $\kappa_d(n) = O(n)$.
Notice that this argument holds for all $k$ with same $f_n$.
Hence, we proved that the set of all polynomials is dense in $C_0^oo(K)$.

On $C_0^oo(\Omega)$, consider a sequence of compact sets $(K_n)_n$ such that
$K_n\uparrow\Omega$ as $n->oo$. The topology on $C_0^oo(\Omega)$ consists of
all seminorms $p_{n,\alpha}$ by
$$
    p_{n,\alpha}(\phi) = \sup_{x\in K_n}|D^\alpha\phi(x)|.
$$
Hence, the set of all polynomials is also dense in $C_0^oo(\Omega)$.
Now one can obviously take a countable collection of polynomials which is still
dense in $C_0^oo(\Omega)$.
